 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:59:03.457099Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable**.\n\nUnder the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006, a landlord cannot seize a tenant's belongings to pressure payment of rent. Rent arrears are enforced through an application to the Landlord and Tenant Board (s. 69), and only the Board can order eviction or money. Ontario also abolished the landlord's right to distrain (seize goods for rent) in residential tenancies. A term that lets the landlord remove and hold the tenant's property until arrears are paid is therefore a self-help remedy the Act does not allow. Under s. 19, a tenancy agreement provision is void to the extent it contravenes the Act or purports to waive a tenant's rights under it. The clause is void regardless of the tenant's agreement to it.\n\nI'm confident in the outcome and in the Board-application route, but I'm not certain of the exact section number. Please verify s. 19 against the current consolidated text of the Act on e-Laws before relying on it.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 19",
